---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Semantic Search"
  description="How the AI semantic search on seanreardon.com works — self-hosted MiniLM, zero third-party data flow, aggregate-only query logging."
  searchable={true}
>
  {/* `data-pagefind-body` scope-limits Pagefind to this article (excludes
      nav/footer/page-title). Same pattern as about-me, privacy, and the
      lessons-learned post route. See BaseLayout's `searchable` prop docstring
      for the corpus rule. */}
  <article class="lab-feature" data-pagefind-body>
    <p class="lead">
      Type a phrase. The site finds the writing and pages most semantically related to it —
      even when the words don't match exactly.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-it-does">What it does</h2>

    <p>
      This site ships two search surfaces:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Standard search</strong> (the magnifying-glass icon in the header) does
        exact-word matching. Best when you remember the wording you're looking for.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>AI semantic search</strong> finds content by <em>meaning</em>. A query for
        "going beyond what the law requires" surfaces the privacy-compliance post even though
        those exact words don't appear in it. A query for "what AI changes about the developer's
        role" surfaces the AI-integration retrospective even though that post never uses the
        phrase "developer's role."
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      They're complementary, not competing. The standard search wins on precision when you
      remember the words; the AI search wins on recall when you don't.
    </p>

    <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>

    <p>
      Most "AI search" implementations route every query to OpenAI, Anthropic, or a hosted
      vector database. That's a privacy posture, a cost commitment, and a vendor dependency
      bundled together. Here, the architecture is simpler — and entirely on this site's own
      infrastructure:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>At build time</strong>, every published post and reference page (about, privacy,
        this page) gets embedded into a 384-dimensional vector by
        <code>all-MiniLM-L6-v2</code> — a small open-source sentence-embedding model from
        Hugging Face, 22M parameters, MIT-licensed. The vectors are written to a static JSON
        file that ships alongside the rest of the site's assets.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>At query time</strong>, your query is sent to a small Node service on this
        site's server. That service runs the same MiniLM model resident in memory, embeds your
        query, and compares it against every page vector via cosine similarity. The top 10
        results come back as JSON: title, URL, snippet, similarity score.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>That's it.</strong> No vector database. No third-party AI API. No tracking
        pixels. About 260 MB of resident memory on the server, well under the 20% per-site
        budget on the shared VPS.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="why-server-side">Why server-side, not client-side</h2>

    <p>
      Client-side embedding (running MiniLM in the visitor's browser via
      <code>transformers.js</code>) was tempting — zero server cost, perfect privacy by
      construction. It was rejected after a careful look at the first-query UX: client-side
      embedding means the visitor's browser downloads ~80 MB of model files on first search,
      with a cold-start time anywhere from 3 to 60 seconds depending on connection speed.
      That's a poor first impression on a search feature.
    </p>

    <p>
      Server-side means the model is warm-loaded once per process and stays resident.
      First-query latency in the ~50 ms range. The privacy story is preserved by other means:
      the query reaches a server that the site owner controls, doesn't leave that server, and
      isn't logged in any form that could identify the visitor (see below).
    </p>

    <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>

    <p>
      Search queries don't leave this site's infrastructure. No third-party AI provider
      (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, etc.) is involved. The local query log is aggregated and
      anonymized — each log entry contains the query text, the number of results returned,
      the top result's identifier, and a timestamp truncated to the hour. <strong>No IP, no
      session, no user identifier.</strong>
    </p>

    <p>
      The full breakdown is in the <a href="/privacy/#what-is-collected">privacy policy</a>.
      No consent step is required for semantic search because no personal information is
      processed.
    </p>

    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>

    <p>
      The dedicated AI-search interface — a separate trigger from the standard header search —
      ships in a follow-up release. Until then, the standard keyword search lives at
      <a href="/search/">/search/</a> and is reachable from the magnifying-glass icon in
      the header on every page.
    </p>

    <h2 id="retrospective">Retrospective</h2>

    <p>
      A longer write-up on building this — the model choice, the corpus split between search
      and a future chat feature, the per-site resource budget, the deployment topology — is in
      the queue and will land at <a href="/lessons-learned/">Lessons Learned</a> once it's
      drafted.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lab-feature {
    max-width: 70ch;
  }
  .lab-feature .lead {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--color-fg);
  }
  .lab-feature h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }
  .lab-feature code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9375em;
  }
</style>
